<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odysseas Androutsos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1788–1825</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odysseas Androutsos was a Greek military leader of the War of Independence, active in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>. He won fame by holding off a much larger Ottoman force at the inn of Gravia in 1821, but his independent conduct brought him into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a> and the provisional government. Accused of treason, he was imprisoned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a> and killed there in 1825.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odysseas-androutsos.jpg" alt="Odysseas Androutsos" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Friedel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odysseas_Androutsos_by_Adam_Friedel_1830.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
